The advert in the window was hand-written and slightly crooked: 'Night baker's assistant wanted. No experience necessary. Must not mind early mornings.' I was nineteen, back home for the summer and badly in need of money for my second year at university. Early mornings, I reasoned, could not be that bad. I had, after all, survived several nine o'clock lectures.

{1}

The first week was a blur of exhaustion. I slept badly during the day, ate at strange hours and, on one occasion, fell asleep on the bus home and woke up at the end of the line. At work I was clumsy and slow. I dropped trays, forgot which dough needed to rest and which was ready for the oven, and once managed to set fire to a tea towel.

{2}

From then on, he began to talk while we worked. He told me about the village in southern Italy where his father had taught him to bake, about the years of sixteen-hour days when he first opened the shop, and about the customers who had been buying the same loaf every morning for three decades. Gradually, I realised that he was not only telling stories; he was teaching me.

{3}

By the middle of August, I was trusted to prepare the morning's rolls on my own. There is a particular satisfaction in watching the first customers arrive at six o'clock and leave with something you have made while the rest of the town was asleep. Some of them even began to recognise me, and one elderly woman told me that my rolls were 'almost as good as his', which I chose to take as a compliment.

{4}

Then, in the last week of the summer, Mr Ferraro did not come to work. His daughter phoned at three o'clock to say that he had been taken to hospital with chest pains and that the doctors wanted to keep him in for a few days. The shop, she said, would have to close.

{5}

That night was the longest of my life. I worked alone, checking and rechecking every step, talking to the dough as I had heard him do a hundred times. When the first customers arrived, the shelves were full. Nobody seemed to notice that anything was different, which was exactly what I had hoped for.

{6}

I went back to university in September, and I have not worked in a bakery since. But I still wake up before dawn more often than I would like, and when I do, I think of that warm, bright room in the middle of the dark street, and of the man who taught me that some things can only be learnt with your hands.

My uncle Tomás has run a hardware shop in the same narrow street for thirty-one years. It sells everything from screws to garden chairs, and he can find any item in seconds, although nobody else can. Last summer, after a large DIY chain opened on the edge of town, his takings fell by almost a third. At a family lunch, someone suggested, half-jokingly, that what the shop needed was a website. Everyone turned to look at me.

{1}

My uncle was sceptical from the start. He did not see why anyone would buy a hammer without holding it first, and he was deeply suspicious of anything that involved passwords. Still, he agreed to let me try, on the condition that I did not 'change anything' in the shop itself.

{2}

The website itself took less time than I expected. I chose a simple design, wrote short descriptions for the two hundred most popular items and added a button that allowed customers to reserve products online and collect them from the shop. I deliberately avoided home delivery: neither of us had any idea how to organise it, and the costs would have wiped out any profit.

{3}

Not one of them placed an order. For the first fortnight, in fact, nothing happened at all. Then, slowly, reservations started to arrive: a set of shelves, a particular kind of light bulb, a replacement part for a forty-year-old coffee machine that apparently nobody else in the region stocked. That last order taught me something important. The shop could never compete with the chain on price, but it could win on the things the chain did not sell.

{4}

By Christmas, around a fifth of the shop's sales started online. More surprisingly, many of the people who came to collect their reservations stayed to browse, and left with more than they had ordered. My uncle, who had once refused to touch the computer, began checking the reservations every morning before he opened the shutters.

{5}

That video brought in customers from all over the region, but the shop is not saved, exactly. The chain store is still there, and my uncle still worries about the rent. But the takings have recovered to where they were before it opened, and for the first time in years he talks about the future rather than about retirement.

{6}

My tutor's description was accurate, though perhaps not in the way she intended. When people ask what I learnt from the experience, they usually expect me to talk about technology. In fact, the most valuable lesson had nothing to do with computers. A small business survives not by imitating its bigger competitors, but by doing well the things they cannot be bothered to do at all.

I had been looking at the mountain for most of my life. From my grandparents' kitchen window it filled the whole horizon, grey in winter and green for a few short weeks in spring, and every summer I promised myself that this would be the year I climbed it. Every summer, something got in the way. When I finally set off, at the age of twenty, I had read every guide and watched every video I could find. I was, I believed, thoroughly prepared.

{1}

Above the hut, I continued alone. The path climbed steeply through pine forest and then out onto open slopes, where the wind arrived without warning and the temperature dropped by several degrees in a matter of minutes. My rucksack, which had seemed so reassuringly full that morning, now felt as though it had been packed with stones. I had planned to reach the summit by midday. By midday, I had barely covered half the distance.

{2}

I remembered something my grandfather had told me years earlier: on a mountain, the slowest walker in the group should set the speed. I had always assumed this was simply good manners. Now I understood that it was practical advice. I shortened my steps, matched them to my breathing and, to my astonishment, stopped needing to rest.

{3}

I would like to say that I followed that advice immediately. In fact, I spent several minutes arguing with myself. The summit was perhaps forty minutes away. I had waited years for this. Surely I could find my way? It was the thought of explaining to my grandfather that I had got lost that finally made me turn back.

{4}

He did not ask whether I had reached the top. Instead, he handed me a bowl of soup and asked what the mountain had been like. I told him about the wind, the older couple, the cloud. He listened without interrupting, and when I had finished he said only that I had done well to turn back.

{5}

I climbed it the following summer, on a clear day in July, walking at the pace of my own breath. The view from the top was everything I had imagined. And yet, oddly, it is not the summit I remember most clearly, but the moment in the cloud when I chose to turn around.

{6}

My grandfather died two years ago. Among his things, we found a small notebook in which he had recorded every climb he had ever made, including, in his careful handwriting, the date I first set off: 'Turned back. Good decision.' Underneath, in different ink, he had added the date of my second attempt, and a single word: 'Patience.'
